u some idea of what  the underlying function is and most of  the options that work for the underlying  function should also work for the  complex variant of that plot and in  cases where we need it to do something  special with the input then we've added  new options for these plots and I will I  will point them out to you as we go  along okay so with that I can jump into  the functions the first one is complex  lists plot and it's probably the  simplest one and it takes in a list of  complex numbers and it plots them on the  axes so here we generate a table of  complex numbers by plugging in some  values into the zeta function and plot  the real part on the x-axis and the  imaginary part on the y-axis and so we  get this familiar looking plot of the of  the complex numbers along the critical  line so complex lists plot is built on  top of list plot and so you can use  options like joined and scaling  functions and they will still behave  like you would expect them to  so the next two plots are re-employed  and abs art plot and these plots take a  complex functions and and do a few  things with them to plot them so reading  plot plots the both the real and the  imaginary parts of the function so while  plot of a function like this will just  give you the real part of the of the  function re-employed plots both the real  and the imaginary parts and it styles  them differently and so if you look at  something like plot legends this  automatically knows that there are two  parts to this plot and it splits the  legend you know nicely for you to be  able to differentiate between the two  parts and I'm also overlaying these  plots with the with the roots of the  zeta function using epilogue but the two  options to note here are real Abel's an  dream style which are new options for  re-employed which give you better  control over styling and labeling both  the parts that are the outputs to this  function  abs are plot we'll take the function and  it'll plot the absolute value of the  function and then it will color the  function based on its arguments so if  you look at the legend on the right side  of the plot it will give you the key to  read the color that is bleeped being  plotted in the plot so so the bottom  function with plots Ahriman Z as you saw  in this one it's for the for this  specific int but it's purely it's purely  real and so there is no imaginary part  to that function and so that's probably  a bit easier to read here there only two  colors going on and you can pick the  colors and figure out what the argument  is for that plot the top one is  obviously the zeta function which has  more variation next plot is complex plot  this is a plot that is on a complex  plane and it takes the complex function  and plot set and and it does a couple of  things so first let's talk about the  color it colors the function based on  its argument obviously you know just  like for apps apps art plot you can read  off the argument based on the color if  you have this kind of a coloring scheme  it's so happens that we can also pick  out some important features of the plot  based on how the color changes on any  given point and so that's what the  thumbnails across the top are showing  you so there are a few features I did  silly if we look at the first one which  is a 0 so that arrow the white arrow is  showing you the direction in which the  colors are changing for this particular  color scheme so if we start off with the  color that is corresponding to  the minimum of the argument on the  legend the right-hand side of the legend  and then go around starts off with that  sort of turquoise ee sort of color and  then it goes all the way around to the  cyan ish car and so if you look at the  legend you can see the both endpoints  the bottom and the top and then if we  figure out which way in which direction  the colors are changing so that's sort  of changing in the counter clockwise in  which tells you that there is zero at  that point a